## Exact main result

The state contains scientific data, a journal, and a mutable engine. The engine
body can work, search for another engine, or install an engine; installed code
begins on the next round after the current finite body finishes. Subsequent
engines may themselves search and install. The environment is one arbitrary
total deterministic function of request and Core, shared between the compared
executions. It cannot depend directly on padding, elapsed cost, or engine code.

Removing current padding preserves Core and code. Structural simulation proves
preservation of the observable round-boundary trace and no increase in future
cost, despite arbitrary shared future engine replacements. At every positive
horizon the saving is at least the initial padding. Therefore the computable
guard `2 + code.size < padding` pays the declared initial change cost and
strictly improves `reward(trace) - cost`, for every fixed integer-valued trace
reward. The desired improvement is derived, not assumed.

`verified_change` connects acceptance by the actual `tryChange` function to
both branches' inductive execution relation, exact installation/continuation
cost accounting, identical observations, and strict utility improvement.

## Gluing

Gluing supplies a genuine pushout of types and functions, including existence,
uniqueness, necessity of compatibility, and predicate descent. Continuation
uses that pushout to descend the actual transition and factor observable
traces. Its quotient is proved equivalent to Core times Code, so precisely
padding and component identity are forgotten. Cost cannot descend to this
quotient, as explicitly proved; its comparison is correctly handled separately.
No model category, cofibration, opfibration, or dependent declaration gluing is
claimed by the final documentation.

## Positive and missing-condition examples

The example with two subsequent engine changes has incumbent cost 33 and
changed cost 26, including initial change cost 5. Its saving does not rely on
freezing the incumbent. Other checked examples show the importance of positive
horizon, common responses, compatible bodies, ordered padding, a fixed cost-
penalizing objective, and charging the initial change. A short-lived saving of
2 is defeated by a change cost of 4 after the engines coalesce. Six additional
cost units remain affordable for the main example; seven give equality of
total costs and eight reverse the comparison. A rejected small-padding case
can still improve at a longer chosen horizon, so the guard is sufficient and
not a complete profitability decision procedure.

## Scope and remaining mathematics

The theorem concerns one padding-removal transformation in a finite engine
language, compared over positive finite whole-round horizons, with the stated
deterministic cost-independent environment and reward-minus-abstract-cost
objective. Costs are the declared mathematical unit costs. The theorem is
available at the comparison baseline; this is not a bound on actual Lean
proof checking time. A separate result permits an additional explicitly
bounded expense. Deadlines concern completed round observations.

The final documentation accurately states that arbitrary interpreter changes,
time-sensitive or stochastic environments, first-proof reciprocal-time utility,
infinite-horizon utility, and dependent proof-context attachment need further
theorems. The defined transition relation is not asserted to equal every action
of the external research controller. These scope limits do not invalidate the
completed conditional self-improvement theorem and do not require postponing
the Agoh/Markov mathematical work.
